\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Chapter 8
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Normative Theory of Equilibrium: Welfare Properties
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
8.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the preceding chapter, we studied the conditions which would guarantee
 the existence, uniqueness, and stability of competitive equilibrium.
 In this chapter, we will study the welfare properties of competitive equilibriu
m.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Economists are interested not only in describing the world of the market
 economy, but also in evaluating it.
 Does a competitive market do a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

good
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 job in allocating resources? Adam Smith's 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

invisible hand
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 says that market economy is efficient.
 Then, in what sense and under what conditions is a market efficient? The
 concept of efficiency is related to a concern with the well-being of those
 in the economy.
 The normative analysis can not only help us understand what advantage the
 market mechanism has, it can also help us to evaluate an economic system
 used in the real world.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Economic efficiency consists of three requirements:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Exchange efficiency: goods are traded efficiently so that no further mutual
 beneficial trade can be obtained.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Production efficiency: there is no waste of resources in producing goods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Product mix efficiency, i.e., the mix of goods produced by the economy reflects
 the preferences of those in the economy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
8.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Pareto Efficiency of Allocation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When economists take about the efficiency of allocating resources, it means
 Pareto efficiency.
 It provides a minimum criterion for efficiency of using resources.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under the market institution, we want to know what is the relationship between
 a market equilibrium and Pareto efficiency.
 There are two basic questions: (1) If a market (not necessarily competitive)
 equilibrium exists, is it Pareto efficient? (2) Can any Pareto efficient
 allocaiton be obtianed through the market mechanism by redistributing endowment
s?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The concept of Pareto efficiency is not just to study the efficiency of
 a market economy, but it also can be used to study the efficiency of any
 economic system.
 First, let us define the notion of Pareto improvement.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
8.2.1 (Pareto Improvement).
 
\series default
An allocation can have a Pareto improvement if it is feasible and there
 is another feasible allocation such that one person would be better off
 and all other persons are not worse off.
 An allocation can have a strict Pareto improvement if it is feasible and
 there is another feasible allocaiton such that all persons are better off.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When economists talk about the efficiency of allocation of resources, it
 means Pareto efficiency.
 It provides a minimum criterion for efficiency of using resources.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
8.2.2 (Pareto Efficiency, also called Pareto Optimality).

\series default
 An allocation is Pareto efficient or Pareto optimal (PO) if it is feasible
 and there is no Pareto improvement, that is, if there is no other feasible
 allocation such that one person would be better off and all other persons
 are not worse off.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*
More precisely, for exchange economies, a feasible allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is Pareto optimal if there is no other allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 such that
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}^{\prime}\leq{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}w_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i:x_{i}^{\prime}\succsim_{i}x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ k:x_{k}^{\prime}\succ_{k}x_{k}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Definition*
For a production economy, 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)$
\end_inset

 is Pareto optimal if and only if:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}\leq\hat{y}+\hat{w}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
There is no feasible allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{\prime},y^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\forall\ i:x_{i}^{\prime}\succsim_{i}x_{i}\\
\exists\ k:x_{k}^{\prime}\succ_{k}x_{k}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
A weaker concept about economic efficiency is the so-called weak Pareto
 efficiency.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
8.2.3 (Weak Pareto Efficiency).

\series default
 An allocation is weakly Pareto efficient if it is feasible and there is
 no strict Pareto improvement, that is, if there is no other feasible allocation
 such that all persons are better off.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 8.2.1.

\series default
 Some textbooks have used weak Pareto optimality as the definition of Pareto
 optimality.
 Under which conditions are they equivalent? It is clear that Pareto efficiency
 implies weak Pareto efficiency.
 But the coverse may not be true.
 However, under the continuity and strict monotonicity of preferences, the
 converse is true.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
\begin_inset Formula $PI(x)=\{x^{\prime}|\ \forall\ i\in N:x_{i}^{\prime}\succsim x_{i},\ \exists\ j\in N:x_{j}^{\prime}\succ_{j}x_{j}\}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is a Pareto optimal if 
\begin_inset Formula $PI(x)=\emptyset$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $SPI(x)=\{x^{\prime}|\ \forall\ i\in N:x_{i}^{\prime}\succ_{i}x_{i}\}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is weakly Pareto optimal if 
\begin_inset Formula $SPI(x)=\emptyset$
\end_inset

.
 Pareto optimality implies weak Pareto optimality, but in general, weak
 Pareto optimality does not imply Pareto optimality.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg(\forall\ i\in N:x_{i}^{\prime}\succ_{i}x_{i})$
\end_inset

 is weaker than 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg(\forall\ i\in N:x_{i}^{\prime}\succsim x_{i},\ \exists\ j\in N:x_{j}^{\prime}\succ_{j}x_{j})$
\end_inset

.
 Not satisfying a strong condition is weaker than not satisfying a weak
 condition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
8.2.1.
 
\series default
\emph on
Under the continuity and strict monotonicity of preferences, weak Pareto
 efficiency implies Pareto efficiency.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Suppose for contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is weakly Pareto efficient, but not Pareto efficient.
 Then, there exists a feasible allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i:x_{i}^{\prime}\succsim_{i}x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ k:x_{k}^{\prime}\succ_{k}x_{k}$
\end_inset

.
 Define the allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}$
\end_inset

 as follows
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\bar{x}_{k} & = & (1-\theta)x_{k}^{\prime}\\
\bar{x}_{i} & = & x_{i}^{\prime}+\frac{\theta}{n-1}x_{k}^{\prime};\ i\neq k
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Then, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\bar{x}_{k}+\sum_{i\neq k}\bar{x}_{i} & = & (1-\theta)x_{k}^{\prime}+\sum_{i\neq k}\left(x_{i}^{\prime}+\frac{\theta}{n-1}x_{k}^{\prime}\right)\\
 & = & (1-\theta)x_{k}^{\prime}+{\displaystyle \sum_{i\neq k}}x_{i}^{\prime}+\frac{\theta}{(n-1)}{\displaystyle \sum_{i\neq k}}x_{k}^{\prime}\nonumber \\
 & = & x_{k}^{\prime}-\theta x_{k}^{\prime}+{\displaystyle \sum_{i\neq k}}x_{i}^{\prime}+\frac{\theta}{(n-1)}(n-1)x_{k}^{\prime}\nonumber \\
 & = & \sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{\prime}\nonumber 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

which means 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}$
\end_inset

 is feasible.
 By continuity of preferences 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{k}=(1-\theta)x_{k}^{\prime}\succ_{k}x_{k}$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\approx0$
\end_inset

.
 By strict monotonicity, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{i}\gneq x_{i}^{\prime}\implies\bar{x}_{i}\succ_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i\neq k$
\end_inset

.
 By transitivity, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{i}\succ_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\ \wedge\ x_{i}^{\prime}\succsim_{i}x_{i}\implies\bar{x}_{i}\succ_{i}x_{i}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i\neq k$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}$
\end_inset

 is a strict Pareto improvement of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 However, this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is weakly Pareto optimal.
 Therefore, we must have every weak Pareto efficient allocation is Pareto
 efficient under the monotonicity and continuity of preferences.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 8.2.2.

\series default
 The trick of taking a little from one person and then equally distributing
 it to the others, so it will make every one better off, is a useful one.
 We will use the same trick to prove the Second Theorem of Welfare Economics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 8.2.3.

\series default
 The above proposition also depends on an implicit assumption that the goods
 under consideration are all private goods.
 Tian (Economics Letters, 1988) showed by example that if goods are public
 goods, we may now not have such equivalence between Pareto efficiency and
 weak Pareto efficiency under the monotonicity and continuity of preferences.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 8.2.4.

\series default
 
\emph on
Equity
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
Pareto efficiency
\emph default
 are two different concepts.
 The points like 
\begin_inset Formula $O_{A}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $O_{B}$
\end_inset

 are Pareto optimal points, but these are extremely unequal.
 To make an allocation be relatively equitable and also efficient, government
 needs to implement some institutional arrangements such as taxes and/or
 subsidies to balance between equity and efficiency, but this is a value
 judgement and policy issue.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 8.2.5.

\series default
 The set of Pareto efficient allocations is given by the contract curve.
 We will show that when Pareto efficient points are given by tangent points
 of two persons' indifferences curves, it should satisfy the following condition
s:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
-\frac{dx_{A2}}{dx_{A1}}=MRS_{x_{1},x_{2}}^{A}=\frac{MU_{x_{1}}^{A}}{MU_{x_{2}}^{A}}=\frac{MU_{x_{1}}^{B}}{MU_{x_{2}}^{B}}=MRS_{x_{1},x_{2}}^{B}=-\frac{dx_{B2}}{dx_{B1}}
\]

\end_inset

When indifference curves of two agents are never tangent, we have the following
 cases.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $n=2=L$
\end_inset

.
 For linear indifference curves with non-zero (negative) slope, indifference
 curves of agents may not be tangent.
 When indifference curves are linear and Person B's indifference curves
 are steeper, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $MRS_{x_{1},x_{2}}^{A}<MRS_{x_{1},x_{2}}^{B}$
\end_inset

, then the set of Pareto efficient allocations is given by the upper and
 left edges of the Edgeworth box.
 When indifference curves are linear and Person A's indifferences curves
 are steeper, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $MRS_{x_{1},x_{2}}^{A}>MRS_{x_{1},x_{2}}^{B}$
\end_inset

, then the set of Pareto efficient allocations is given by the lower and
 right edges of the Edgeworth box.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
Suppose that indifference curves are given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{A}(x_{A})=x_{A2}
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{B}(x_{B})=x_{B1}
\]

\end_inset

So, both individuals only care about one commodity.
 Then, the only Pareto efficient allocation is the upper left corner point
 of the Edgeworth Box.
 However, the set of weakly Pareto efficient points is given by the upper
 and left borders of the box.
 Notice that utility functions in this example are continuous and monotonic,
 but a weak Pareto efficient allocation may not be Pareto efficient.
 This example shows that strict monotonicity cannot be replaced by monotonicity
 for the equivalence of Pareto efficiency and weak Pareto efficiency.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
Now suppose that indifference curves are perfect complementary.
 Then, utility functions are given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{A}(x_{A})=\min\{ax_{A1},bx_{A2}\}
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{B}(x_{B})=\min\{cx_{B1},dx_{B2}\}
\]

\end_inset

A special case is the one where 
\begin_inset Formula $a=b=c=d=1$
\end_inset

.
 Then, the set of Pareto optimal allocaitons is the are given by points
 between two 
\begin_inset Formula $45^{\circ}$
\end_inset

 lines.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
One person's indifference curves are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

thick.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 In this case, a weak Pareto efficient allocation may not be Pareto efficient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
8.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The First Fundamental Theorem of Welfare Economics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There is a well-known theorem, in fact, one of the most important theorems
 in Economics, which characterizes a desirable nature of the competitive
 market institution.
 It claims that every competitive equilibrium allocation is Pareto efficient.
 A remarkable part of this theorem is that the theorem requires few assumpeitons
, much fewer than those for the existence of competitive equilibrium.
 Some implicit assumptions in this section are that preferences are orderings,
 goods are divisible, and there are no public goods or externalities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
8.3.1 (The First Fundamental Theorem of Welfare Economics).

\series default
 
\emph on
If 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y,p)$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium, then 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)$
\end_inset

 is weakly Pareto efficient, and further under local non-satiation, it is
 Pareto efficient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y,p)$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium.
 Suppose for contradiction 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)$
\end_inset

 is not weakly Pareto optimal.
 Then, there exists a feasible allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{\prime},y^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N:x_{i}^{\prime}\succ_{i}x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 By utility maximization, we must have 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N:p\cdot x_{i}^{\prime}>p\cdot w_{i}+{\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{J}}\theta_{ij}p\cdot y_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Taking summation we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{i=1}^{N}p\cdot x_{i}^{\prime}>\sum_{i=1}^{N}p\cdot w_{i}+\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j=1}^{J}\theta_{ij}p\cdot y_{j}
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}$
\end_inset

 is a profit maximizing vector, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y_{j}\geq p\cdot y_{j}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 So, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
\sum_{i=1}^{N}p\cdot x_{i}^{\prime}>\sum_{i=1}^{N}p\cdot w_{i}+\sum_{j=1}^{J}p\cdot y_{j}^{\prime}\\
p\cdot\left[\sum_{i=1}^{N}x_{i}^{\prime}-\sum_{i=1}^{N}w_{i}-\sum_{j=1}^{J}y_{j}^{\prime}\right]>0
\end{gather}

\end_inset

However, this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{\prime},y^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is feasible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To show Pareto efficiency, suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)$
\end_inset

 is not Pareto optimal.
 Then, there exists a feasible allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{\prime},y^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N:x_{i}^{\prime}\succsim_{i}x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ k\in N:x_{k}^{\prime}\succ_{k}x_{k}$
\end_inset

.
 We claim that by local non-satiation, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N:p\cdot x_{i}^{\prime}\geq p\cdot w_{i}+\sum_{j=1}^{J}\theta_{ij}p\cdot y_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose the claim is not true.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ i\in N:p\cdot x_{i}^{\prime}<p\cdot w_{i}+\sum_{j=1}^{J}\theta_{ij}p\cdot y_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 This means that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}\in\mbox{Int}(B_{i}(p))$
\end_inset

, which is an open set by definition.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ \epsilon>0:O_{\epsilon}(x_{i}^{\prime})\subseteq B_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

.
 By local non-satiation, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ x_{i}^{\prime\prime}\in O_{\epsilon}(x_{i})\subseteq B_{i}(p):x_{i}^{\prime\prime}\succ_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 By transitivity, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime\prime}\succ_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\succsim x_{i}$
\end_inset

 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime\prime}\succ_{i}x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 But, this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 maximizes agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's utility.
 Hence, the claim is true.
 Moving on, by utility maximization, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x_{k}^{\prime}>p\cdot w_{k}+\sum_{j=1}^{J}\theta_{kj}p\cdot y_{k}$
\end_inset

.
 Taking summation, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{i=1}^{N}p\cdot x_{i}^{\prime}>\sum_{i=1}^{N}p\cdot w_{i}+\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j=1}^{J}\theta_{ij}p\cdot y_{j}
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}$
\end_inset

 is a profit maximizing vector, then then 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y_{j}\geq p\cdot y_{j}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 So, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\sum_{i=1}^{N}p\cdot x_{i}^{\prime} & > & \sum_{i=1}^{N}p\cdot w_{i}+\sum_{j=1}^{J}p\cdot y_{j}\geq\sum_{i=1}^{N}p\cdot w_{i}+\sum_{j=1}^{J}p\cdot y_{j}^{\prime}\\
\sum_{i=1}^{N}p\cdot x_{i}^{\prime} & > & \sum_{i=1}^{N}p\cdot w_{i}+\sum_{j=1}^{J}p\cdot y_{j}^{\prime}\\
p\cdot\left[\sum_{i=1}^{N}x_{i}^{\prime}-\sum_{i=1}^{N}w_{i}-\sum_{j=1}^{J}y_{j}^{\prime}\right] & > & 0\nonumber 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

However, this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{\prime},y^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is feasible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 8.3.1.
 
\series default
If the local non-satiation condition is not satisfied, a competitive equilibrium
 allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 may not be Pareto optimal, say, for the case of thick indifference curves.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 8.3.2.

\series default
 Note that neither convexity of preferences nor convexity of production
 set is assumed in the theorem.
 The conditions required for Pareto efficiency of competitive equilibrium
 is much weaker than the conditions for the existence of competitive equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 8.3.3.

\series default
 If goods are indivisible, then a competitive equilibrium allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 may not be Pareto optimal.
 Hence, the divisibility condition cannot be dropped for the First Fundamental
 Welfare Theorem to be true.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
8.4
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Calculations of Pareto Optimum by First-Order Conditions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The first-order conditions for Pareto efficiency are a bit harder to formulate
 However, the following trick is very useful.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.4.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Exchange Economies
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
8.4.1.

\series default
 
\emph on
A feasible allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

is Pareto efficient if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

solves the following problem for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,2,\ldots,n$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\max_{x}\ u_{i}(x_{i})\\
\mbox{s.t.}\ \sum x_{k}\leq\sum w_{k}\\
u_{k}(x_{k})\geq u_{k}(x_{k}^{*})\ \mbox{for}\ k\neq i
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 solves all the maximization problems, but 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is not Pareto efficient.
 Then, there exists an allocation such that at least one person is better
 off, but no one else is worse off.
 But, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 doesn't solve one of the maximization problems, which is a contradiction.
 Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is Pareto efficient, but it does not solve one of the maximization problems.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 solve that problem.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 makes one of the agents better off without making anyone else worse off,
 which contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is Pareto efficient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If utility functions 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(x_{i})$
\end_inset

 are differentiable, then we can define the Lagrangian funciton to get the
 optimal solution to the above problem:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L=u_{i}(x_{i})+q(\hat{w}-\hat{x})+\sum_{k\neq i}t_{k}(u_{k}(x_{k})-u_{k}(x_{k}^{*}))
\]

\end_inset

The first-order conditions are given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{il}} & = & \frac{\partial u_{i}(x_{i})}{\partial x_{il}}-q_{l}\leq0\ \mbox{with equality if \ensuremath{x_{il}>0;}}\ l=1,\ldots,L,\ i=1,\ldots,n\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{kl}} & = & t_{k}\frac{\partial u_{k}(x_{k})}{\partial x_{kl}}-q_{l}\leq0\ \mbox{with equality if \ensuremath{x_{kl}>0;}}\ l=1,\ldots,L,\ k\neq i
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is an interor solution, then by (6) we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\frac{\partial u_{i}(x_{i})}{\partial x_{il}}}{\frac{\partial u_{i}(x_{i})}{\partial x_{ih}}}=\frac{q_{l}}{q_{h}}=MRS_{x_{il},x_{ih}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and by (7) we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\frac{\partial u_{k}(x_{k})}{\partial x_{kl}}}{\frac{\partial u_{k}(x_{k})}{\partial x_{kh}}}=\frac{q_{l}}{q_{h}}=MRS_{x_{kl},x_{kh}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Thus, we have 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
MRS_{x_{1l},x_{1h}}=\cdots=MRS_{x_{nl},x_{nh}};\ l=1,\ldots,L,\ h=1,\ldots,L
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which are necessary conditions for the interior solutions to be Pareto efficient
, which means that the MRS of any two goods are all equal for all agents.
 They become sufficient conditions when utility functions 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(x_{i})$
\end_inset

 are differentiable and quasi-concave.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.4.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Production Economies
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For simplicity, we assume there is only one firm.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $T(y)\leq0$
\end_inset

 be the transformation frontier.
 Similarly, we can prove the following proposition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
8.4.2.

\series default
 
\emph on
A feasible allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},y^{*})$
\end_inset

 is Pareto efficient if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},y^{*})$
\end_inset

 solves the folowing problem for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,2,\ldots,n$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\max_{x}\ u_{i}(x_{i})\\
\mbox{s.t.}\ \sum_{k\in N}x_{k}=\sum_{k\in N}w_{k}+y\\
u_{k}(x_{k})\geq u_{k}(x_{k}^{*})\ \mbox{for}\ k\neq i\\
T(y)\leq0
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If utility functions 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(x_{i})$
\end_inset

 are differenetiable and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is an interior solution, then we can define the Lagrangian function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L=u_{i}(x_{i})-\lambda T(\hat{x}-\hat{w})+\sum_{k\neq i}t_{k}\left[u_{k}(x_{k})-u_{k}(x_{k}^{*})\right]
\]

\end_inset

The first-order conditions are given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{il}} & = & \frac{\partial u_{i}(x_{i})}{\partial x_{il}}-\lambda_{}\frac{\partial T(y)}{\partial y_{l}}\leq0\ \mbox{with equality if \ensuremath{x_{il}>0;}}\ l=1,\ldots,L,\ i=1,\ldots,n\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{kl}} & = & t_{k}\frac{\partial u_{k}(x_{k})}{\partial x_{kl}}-\lambda\frac{\partial T(y)}{\partial y_{l}}\leq0\ \mbox{with equality if \ensuremath{x_{kl}>0;}}\ l=1,\ldots,L,\ k\neq i
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is an interior solution, then by (11) we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\frac{\partial u_{i}(x_{i})}{\partial x_{il}}}{\frac{\partial u_{i}(x_{i})}{\partial x_{ih}}}=\frac{\frac{\partial T(y)}{\partial y_{l}}}{\frac{\partial T(y)}{\partial y_{h}}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and by (12) we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\frac{\partial u_{k}(x_{k})}{\partial x_{kl}}}{\frac{\partial u_{k}(x_{k})}{\partial x_{kh}}}=\frac{\frac{\partial T(y)}{\partial y_{l}}}{\frac{\partial T(y)}{\partial y_{h}}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Thus, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
MRS_{x_{1l},x_{1h}}=\cdots=MRS_{x_{nl},x_{nh}}=MRTS_{y_{l},y_{h}};\ l=1,\ldots,L,\ h=1,\ldots,L
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which are the necessary conditions for the interior solutions to be Pareto
 efficient, which means that the MRS of any two goods for all agents equals
 the MRTS.
 They become sufficient conditions when utility functions 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(x_{i})$
\end_inset

 are differentiable and quasi-concave and the production functions are concave.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
8.5
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Second Fundamental Theorem of Welfare Economics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, we can assert a converse of the First Fundamental Theorem of Welfare
 Economics.
 The Second Fundamental Theorem of Welfare Economics gives conditions under
 which every Pareto optimum allocation can be 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

supported
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 by a competitive equilibrium through a redistribution of endowments so
 that one does not need to seek any alternative economic institution to
 reach Pareto efficient allocations.
 Under its assumptions, it tells us that any desired Pareto optimal allocation
 can be achieved as a market-based equilibrium with transfers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We first define the competitive equilibrium with transfer payments (also
 called an equilibrium relative to a price system), that is, a competitive
 equilibrium is established after transferring some of the initial endowments
 between agents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
8.5.1 (Competitive Equilibrium with Transfer Payments).

\series default
 For an economy, 
\begin_inset Formula $e=(e_{1},\ldots,e_{n},\{Y_{j}\})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y,p)\in X\times Y\times\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium with transfer payment if
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\succsim_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}\in\left\{ x_{i}^{\prime}\in X_{i}:p\cdot x_{i}^{\prime}\leq p\cdot x_{i}\right\} $
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset

 (utility maximization)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y_{j}\geq p\cdot y_{j}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}^{\prime}\in Y_{j}$
\end_inset

 (profit maximization)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}\leq\hat{w}+\hat{y}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 8.5.1.

\series default
 An equilibrium with transfer payments is different from a comeptitive equilibri
um with respect to a budget constrained utility maximization that uses a
 value calculated from the initial endowment.
 An equilibrium with transfer payments is defined without reference to the
 distribution of initial endowment, given the total amount.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
Transfer payments occur before the agent compete and/or trade in the market.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 8.5.2.

\series default
 If condition (i) above is replaced by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{(i')}\ x_{i}^{\prime}\succ_{i}x_{i}\ \mbox{implies that}\ p\cdot x_{i}^{\prime}\geq p\cdot x_{i}\ \mbox{for}\ i=1,\ldots,n
\]

\end_inset

then 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y,p)\in X\times Y\times\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 is called a price quasi-equilibrium with transfer payment.
 It may be remarked that, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{w}>0$
\end_inset

 and preferences are continuous and locally non-satiated, then any price
 quasi-equilibrium with transfers in which 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}>0$
\end_inset

 is a price equilibrium with transfers.
 Furthermore, if preferences are strictly monotone, then we must have 
\begin_inset Formula $p>0$
\end_inset

 in any price quasi-equilibrium with transfers.
 For the proofs, see Mas-Colell, Whinston, and Green (1995).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is a vector of vectors; it is an allocation.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}^{*}$
\end_inset

 represents aggregate demand, it is a vector of numbers.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}^{*}=\sum_{i\in N}\ x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
8.5.1 (The Second Fundamental Theorem of Welfare Economics).

\series default
 
\emph on
Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},y^{*})$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}>0$
\end_inset

 is Pareto optimal, suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 are continuous, convex, and strictly monotonic, and suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{j}$
\end_inset

 are closed and convex.
 Then, there is a price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $p\geq0$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},y^{*},p)$
\end_inset

 is a comeptitive equilibrium with transfer payments, i.e.,
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}\succ_{i}x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x_{i}^{\prime}>p\cdot x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is not feasible for agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 This property is equivalent to (i) in Definition 8.5.1)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y_{j}^{*}\geq p\cdot y_{j}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}^{\prime}\in Y_{j}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j=1,\ldots,J$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(x_{i}^{*})=\left\{ x_{i}\in X_{i}:x_{i}\succ_{i}x_{i}^{*}\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

be the strict upper contour set of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

, and let
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(x^{*})={\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}P(x_{i}^{*})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

be the summation of the strict upper contour sets of all the agents.
 By the convexity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

, we know that 
\begin_inset Formula $P(x_{i}^{*})$
\end_inset

 are all convex sets, so 
\begin_inset Formula $P(x^{*})$
\end_inset

 is also a convex set.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $W=\left\{ \hat{w}\right\} +\sum_{j=1}^{J}Y_{j}$
\end_inset

 which is closed and convex.
 Then, by Pareto optimality of 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},y^{*})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $W\cap P(x^{*})=\emptyset$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, by the Separating Hyperplane Theorem, there is a 
\begin_inset Formula $p\neq0$
\end_inset

 such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\forall\ \hat{x}\in P(x^{*}),\ \forall\ \hat{\sigma}\in W:p\cdot\hat{x}\geq p\cdot\hat{\sigma}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, we show that 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium price vector by the following four steps.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 To see this, let 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{l}=(0,\ldots,0,1,0,\ldots,0)$
\end_inset

 with the 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

-th component one and other places zero.
 Let
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{x}=\hat{\sigma}+e_{l}\ \mbox{for some}\ \hat{\sigma}\in W
\]

\end_inset

 By strict monotonicity and redistribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{l}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}=\hat{\sigma}+e_{l}\in P(x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, by (18), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p\cdot\left(\hat{\sigma}+e_{l}\right)\geq p\cdot\hat{\sigma}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and therefore
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p\cdot e_{l}\geq0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which means
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{l}\geq0,\ l=1,\ldots,L
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ y_{j}\in Y_{j},\ \forall\ j=1,\ldots,J:p\cdot y_{j}^{*}\geq p\cdot y_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}^{*}=\hat{w}+\hat{y}^{*}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\hat{x}^{*}=p\cdot(\hat{w}+\hat{y}^{*})$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\hat{w}=p\cdot\hat{x}^{*}-p\cdot\hat{y}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Also, by (18), 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\hat{x}\geq p\cdot(\hat{w}+\hat{y})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\hat{w}+\hat{y}\right)\in W$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\cdot(\hat{x}-\hat{x}^{*})\geq p\cdot(\hat{y}-\hat{y}^{*})
\]

\end_inset

Letting 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}\rightarrow\hat{x}^{*}$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(\hat{y}-\hat{y}^{*})\leq0
\]

\end_inset

Letting 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{k}=y_{k}^{*}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $k\neq j$
\end_inset

, we have, from the equation above,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall\ y_{j}\in Y_{j}:p\cdot y_{j}^{*}\geq p\cdot y_{j}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We want to show that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\forall\ i=1,\ldots,n:x_{i}\succ_{i}x_{i}^{*}\implies p\cdot x_{i}\geq p\cdot x_{i}^{*}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

To see this, take 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in N$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\succ_{i}x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

, and let
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
x_{i}^{\prime}=(1-\theta)x_{i},\ 0<\theta<1\\
x_{k}^{\prime}=x_{k}^{*}+\frac{\theta}{n-1}x_{i}\ \mbox{for}\ k\neq i
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Then, by continuity of and strict monotonicity of preferences, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall\ i\in N:x_{i}^{\prime}\succ_{i}x_{i}^{*}
\]

\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 sufficiently small.
 Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{x}^{\prime}\in P(x^{*})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}^{*}=\hat{w}+\hat{y}^{*}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\hat{w}+\hat{y}^{*}\right)\in W$
\end_inset

.
 So, by (18), 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\hat{x}^{\prime}\geq p\cdot(\hat{w}+\hat{y}^{*})=p\cdot\hat{x}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Now,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
p\cdot\hat{x}^{\prime} & = & p\cdot\left(x_{i}^{\prime}+{\displaystyle \sum_{k\ne i}}x_{k}^{\prime}\right)\nonumber \\
 & = & p\cdot\left[(1-\theta)x_{i}+{\displaystyle \sum_{k\neq i}}\left(x_{k}^{*}+\frac{\theta}{n-1}x_{i}\right)\right]\nonumber \\
 & = & p\cdot\left[x_{i}+{\displaystyle \sum_{k\neq i}}x_{k}^{*}\right]\nonumber \\
 & \geq & p\cdot\hat{x}^{*}=p\cdot{\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{n}}x_{k}^{*}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p\cdot x_{i}\geq p\cdot x_{i}^{*}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i=1,\ldots,n:x_{i}\succ_{i}x_{i}^{*}\implies p\cdot x_{i}>p\cdot x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 To show this, take 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in N$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\succ_{i}x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 From Step 3, we know that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x_{i}\geq p\cdot x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

, so suppose for contradiction that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p\cdot x_{i}=p\cdot x_{i}^{*}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\succ_{i}x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

, then by the continuity of preferences, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda x_{i}\succ_{i}x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $0<\lambda<1$
\end_inset

 and when 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 is sufficiently close to one.
 By Step 3, we know 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\lambda x_{i}\geq p\cdot x_{i}^{*}=p\cdot x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 This implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\geq1$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x_{i}=p\cdot x_{i}^{*}>0$
\end_inset

.
 However, this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda<1$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x_{i}>p\cdot x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 8.5.3.

\series default
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{w}>0$
\end_inset

, the strict monotonicity condition of preferences can be weakened to local
 non-satiation.
 Also, Mas-Colell, Whinston, and Green (1995) use the price quasi-equilibrium
 concept to present a version of the Second Fundamental Theorem of Welfare
 Economics under much weaker conditions, which only requires the convexities
 of preferences and production possibilities and local non-satiation of
 preferences.
 For the discussion, see Mas-Colell, Whinston, and Green (1995).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For exchange economies, the competitive equilibrium with transfers is the
 same as a regular competitive equilibrium when 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}=x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 As a corollary, we have
\end_layout

\begin_layout Corollary*

\series bold
\shape up
8.5.1.

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}>0$
\end_inset

 be Pareto optimal and suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 are continuous, convex, and strictly monotonic.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium for the initial endowment 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}=x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
Agents don't change their allocations if they start out with what they would
 have chosen optimally.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 8.5.4.
 
\series default
Corollary 8.5.1 states that agents will not change their allocaitons if they
 start out with what they would have chosen optimally.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 8.5.5.

\series default
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 can be represented by a concave and differentiable utility function, then
 the proof of the Second Fundamental Theorem of Welfare Economics can be
 much simpler.
 A sufficient condition for concavitiy is that the Hessian matrix is negative
 definite.
 Also, note that monotonic transformations do not change preferences, so
 we may be able to transform a quasi-concave utility function to a concave
 utility function.
 For example,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u(x,y)=xy
\]

\end_inset

is quasi-concave, but we can use a monotonic transformation to obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u^{\frac{1}{2}}(x,y)=x^{\frac{1}{2}}y^{\frac{1}{2}}
\]

\end_inset

which is concave.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Differentiation Version of the Second Fundamental Theorem of Welfare Economics
 for Exchange Economies
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
If 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}>0$
\end_inset

 is Pareto Optimal, then we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\nabla u_{i}(x_{i}^{*})=q,\ i=1,\ldots,n
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla u_{i}(x_{i}^{*})$
\end_inset

 is the MU of agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 is known as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

shadow price.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Important to note, this is the first-order necessary condition for a Pareto
 optimal allocation.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium price vector.
 To do so, we only need to show that each consumer maximizes his utility
 subject to 
\begin_inset Formula $B(q)=\left\{ x_{i}\in X_{i}:q\cdot x_{i}\leq q\cdot x_{i}^{*}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 Indeed, by the concavity of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{u_{i}(x_{i})-u_{i}(x_{i}^{*})}{x_{i}-x_{i}^{*}} & \leq & \nabla u_{i}(x_{i}^{*})\\
u_{i}(x_{i}) & \leq & u(x_{i}^{*})+\nabla u_{i}(x_{i}^{*})(x_{i}-x_{i}^{*})\nonumber \\
 & = & u(x_{i}^{*})+q(x_{i}-x_{i}^{*})\nonumber 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Since each consumer maximizes his utility subject to his budget constraint,
 then it must be true that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
q\cdot x_{i} & \leq & q\cdot x_{i}^{*}\\
q\cdot(x_{i}-x_{i}^{*}) & \leq & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{i}(x_{i})\leq u_{i}(x_{i}^{*})+q(x_{i}-x_{i}^{*})\leq u_{i}(x_{i}^{*})
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
8.6
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Non-Convex Production Technologies and Marginal Cost Pricing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Convex production sets is an indispensable assumption for the second welfare
 theorem.
 When there are non-convex produciton technologies, the allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 maximizes the welfare of the consumer, but for the only value of relative
 prices that could support 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 as a utility-maximizing bundle, the firm does not maximize profits event
 locally.
 In contrast, the first welfare theorem remains applicable even in the presence
 of non-convexities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As stated previously, the second welfare theorm runs into difficulties in
 the presence of non-convex production sets (here, we do not quesiton the
 assumption of convexity on the consumption side).
 In the first place, large non-convexities caused by the presence of fixed
 costs or extensive increasing returns leads to a world in which there are
 a small number of large firms.
 Consequently, the assumption of price taking becomes less plausible.
 Yet, even if price taking can somehow be relied on, it may still be impossible
 to support a given Pareto optimal allocation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Although non-convexities may prevent us from supporting the Pareto optimal
 production allocation as a profit-maximizing choice, under the differentiabilit
y assumptions on pricing rules of firms, we can use the first-order necessary
 conditions derived there to formulate a weaker result that parallels the
 Second Welfare Theorem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
8.6.1 (The Second Welfare Theorem for Non-Convex Production Sets).

\series default
 
\emph on
Suppose that firm j's transformation function 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{j}(y)$
\end_inset

 is smooth, and all consumers have continuous, monotonic, and convex preferences.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},y^{*})$
\end_inset

 is Pareto optimal, then there exists a price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and wealth levels 
\begin_inset Formula $(I_{1},\ldots,I_{n})$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i}I_{i}=p\cdot\sum_{i}w_{i}+\sum_{j}p\cdot y_{j}^{*}$
\end_inset

 such that:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
For any firm 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p=\gamma_{j}\nabla T_{j}(y^{*})\ \mbox{for some}\ \gamma_{j}>0
\]

\end_inset

(Price is proportional to MC; 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}$
\end_inset

 is a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

; this a firm's choice function not a way to find 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For any 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is maximal for 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 in the budget set
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left\{ x_{i}\in X_{i}:p\cdot x_{i}\le I_{i}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i}x_{i}^{*}=\sum_{i}w_{i}+\sum_{j}y_{j}^{*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The type of equilibrium represented by conditions (i) to (iii) of the above
 theorem is called a 
\series bold
\emph on
marginal cost price equilibrium with transfers
\series default
\emph default
.
 The motivation for this terminology comes from the one-output, one-input
 case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Condition (i) also implies that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{p_{l}}{p_{k}}=\frac{\frac{\partial T_{1}(y)}{\partial y_{l}}}{\frac{\partial T_{1}(y)}{\partial y_{k}}}=\cdots=\frac{\frac{\partial T_{J}(y)}{\partial y_{l}}}{\frac{\partial T_{J}(y)}{\partial y_{k}}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and thus, the marginal rates of technical substitutions, MRTS, of two goods
 are equal for all firms.
 Note, condition (i) is necessary, but not sufficient, for profit maximization,
 and it does not imply that the 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{1}^{*},\ldots,y_{J}^{*})$
\end_inset

 are profit-maximizing production plans for price-taking firms.
 The condition only says that small changes in production plans have no
 first-order effect on profit.
 However, small changes may still have positive second-order effects, and,
 at any rate, large changes may increase profits.
 Thus, to achieve allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},y^{*})$
\end_inset

, it may be required that a regulatory agency prevent the managers of non-convex
 firms from attempting to maximize profits at the given prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It should be noted that the converse result to the above theorem, which
 would assert that every marginal cost price equilibrium 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is Pareto optimal, is not true.
 Informally, this occurs because marginal cost pricing neglects second-order
 conditions and it may therefore happen that, at allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

, the second-order conditions for the social utility maximizaiton problem
 are not satisfied.
 A marginal cost pricing equilibrium need not be a Pareto optimal allocation.
 As a result, satisfaction of the first-order marginal optimality conditions
 does not ensure that the allocation is Pareto optimal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
A marginal cost price equilibrium is not a competitive equilibrium.
 This is because condition (i) is necessary, but not sufficient, for profit
 maximization.
 When production sets are conve, then a MCPE and a CE are equivalent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
8.7
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Pareto Optimality and Social Welfare Maximization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Pareto efficiency is only concerned with efficiency of allocations, but
 it has nothing to say about the distribution of welfare.
 Even if we agree with Pareto optimality, we still do not know which one
 we should be at.
 One way to solve the problem is to assume the existence of a social welfare
 function.
 Define a social welfare function 
\begin_inset Formula $W:X\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $W(u_{1}(x_{1}),\ldots,u_{n}(x_{n}))$
\end_inset

 where we assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $W(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is monotone increasing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Utilitarian Social Welfare Function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\series bold
8.7.1.

\series default
 The utilitarian social welfare function is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
W(u_{1},\ldots,u_{n})={\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}a_{i}u_{i}(x_{i})
\]

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum a_{i}=1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 Note that this is just a weighted average of utilities.
 Under a utilitarian rule, social states are ranked according to the linear
 sum of utilities.
 The utilitarian form is by far the most common and widely applied social
 welfare function in Economics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Rawlsian Social Welfare Function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\series bold
8.7.2.

\series default
 The Rawlsian social welfare function is defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
W(\cdot)=\min\{u_{1}(x_{1}),\ldots,u_{n}(x_{n})\}
\]

\end_inset

So, the utility function is not strictly monotonic increasing.
 The Rawlsian form gives priority to the interests of the worst off members,
 and it is used in the ethical system proposed by Rawls (1971).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.7.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Social Welfare Maximization for Exchange Economies
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We suppose that a society should operate at a point that maximizes social
 welfare; that is, we should choose an allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 solves
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max\ W(u_{1}(x_{1}),\ldots,u_{n}(x_{n}))
\]

\end_inset

subject to
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}\leq\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}
\]

\end_inset

How do the allocations that maximize this welfare funciton compare to Pareto
 efficient allocations? The following is a trivial consequence if the strict
 monotonicity assumption is imposed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
8.7.1.

\series default
 
\emph on
Under strict monotonicity of the social welfare function, 
\begin_inset Formula $W(\cdot)$
\end_inset

, if 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

maximizes a social welfare function, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 must be Pareto optimal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
In general, the set of 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 that maximizes a strictly monotone social welfare function, 
\begin_inset Formula $W(\cdot)$
\end_inset

, is a subset of the set of Pareto optimal allocations.
 
\begin_inset Formula $W(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 can take a variety of forms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 maximizes a social welfare function.
 Suppose for contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is not Pareto optimal.
 Then, there is another feasible allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N:u_{i}(x_{i}^{\prime})\geq u_{i}(x_{i}^{*})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ k:u_{k}(x_{k}^{\prime})>u_{k}(x_{k}^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 Then, by strict monotonicity of 
\begin_inset Formula $W(\cdot)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $W(u_{1}(x_{1}^{\prime}),\ldots,u_{n}(x_{n}^{\prime}))>W(u_{1}(x_{1}^{*}),\ldots,u_{n}(x_{n}^{*}))$
\end_inset

.
 However, this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 maximizes the social welfare function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
8.7.2.

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}>0$
\end_inset

 be a Pareto optimal allocation.
 Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 is concave, differentiable, and strictly monotonic.
 Then, there exists some choice of weights 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 maximizes the welfare function
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
W(u_{1},\ldots,u_{n})=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\alpha_{i}u_{i}(x_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Furthermore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{i}^{*}=\frac{1}{\lambda_{i}}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{i}=\frac{\partial V_{i}(p,I_{i})}{\partial I_{i}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is Pareto optimal, then by the Second Fundamental Theorem of Welfare Economics,
 it is a competitive equilibrium with 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}=x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, the first-order condition of the utility maximization problem tells
 us that at an interior optimum
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\nabla u_{i}(x_{i}^{*})=\lambda_{i}p
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium price vector and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the Lagrange multiplier of consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's budget constraint.
 Also, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{i}$
\end_inset

 represents the marginal change in consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's utility arising from a marginal increase in consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's wealth, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\lambda_{i}=\frac{\partial V_{i}(p,I_{i})}{\partial I_{i}}
\]

\end_inset

which is referred to as consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's marginal utility of wealth.
 Important to note, 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the indirect utility function of consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, for the welfare maximization problem,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max & {\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\alpha_{i}u_{i}(x_{i})\\
\mbox{s.t.} & \sum x_{i}\leq\sum w_{i}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 is concave, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 solves the problem if the first-order condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\alpha_{i}\nabla u_{i}(x_{i})=q;\ i=1,\ldots,n
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is satisfied for some 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

, which is the lagrange multiplier of the constraint.
 Thus, if we let 
\begin_inset Formula $p=q$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{i}^{*}=\frac{1}{\lambda_{i}}$
\end_inset

.
 We know 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 also maximizes the welfare function 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i=1}^{n}\alpha_{i}^{*}u_{i}(x_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Thus, the price is the Lagrangian multiples of the welfare maximization,
 and this measures the marginal social value of the goods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 8.7.1.
 
\series default
To find the set of Pareto optimal allocations, we can use Proposition 8.7.2
 or Propositions 8.4.1/8.4.2.
 These methods are all equivalent since they lead to the same first-order
 conditions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.7.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Welfare Maximization in Production Economy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Define a choice set by the transformation function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
T(\hat{x})=0,\ \hat{x}={\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}
\]

\end_inset

The social wefare maximization problem for the production economy is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\max\ W(u_{1}(x_{i}),\ldots,u_{n}(x_{n}))
\end{equation}

\end_inset

subject to
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
T(\hat{x})=0
\]

\end_inset

Define the Lagrangian function
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
L=W(u_{1}(x_{i}),\ldots,u_{n}(x_{n}))-\lambda T(\hat{x})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Then, the first-order condition is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
W^{\prime}(\cdot)\frac{\partial u_{i}(x_{i})}{\partial x_{il}}-\lambda\frac{\partial T(\hat{x})}{\partial x_{il}}\leq0\ \mbox{with equality if}\ x_{il}>0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and thus, when 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is an interior point, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\frac{\partial u_{i}(x_{i})}{\partial x_{il}}}{\frac{\partial u_{i}(x_{i})}{\partial x_{ik}}}=\frac{\frac{\partial T(\hat{x})}{\partial x_{l}}}{\frac{\partial T(\hat{x})}{\partial x_{k}}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

That is,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
MRS_{x_{il},x_{ik}}=MRTS_{x_{l},x_{k}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The conditions characterizing welfare maximization require that the marginal
 rate of substitution between each pair of commodities must be equal to
 the marginal rate of transformation between the two commodities for all
 agents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
8.8
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Political Overtones
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

By the First Fundamental Theorem of Welfare Economics, implication is that
 what the government should do is to secure the competitive environment
 in an economy and give people full economic freedom.
 So, there should be no subsidizing, no price floor, no price ceiling, stop
 rent control, no regulations, and lift the tax and the import-export barriers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Even if we want to reach a preferred Pareto optimal outcome which may be
 different from a competitive equilibrium from a given endowment, you might
 do so by adjusting the initial endowment but not disturbing prices, imposing
 taxes or regulations.
 That is, if a derive Pareto optimal is not 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fair,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 al the government has to do is to make a lump-sum transfer payment to the
 poor first, keeping the competitive environments intact.
 We can adjust initial endowments to obtain a desired competitive equilibrium
 by the Second Fundamental Theorem of Welfare Economics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Of course, when we reach the above conclusions, you should note that there
 are conditions on the results.
 In many cases, we have market failures, in the sense that either a competitive
 equilibrium does not exist or a competitive equilibrium may not be Pareto
 optimal so that the First or Second Theorem of Welfare Economics cannot
 be applied.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The conditions for the existence of a competitive equilibrium are: (i) convexity
 (diversification of consumption and no IRS), (ii) monotonicity (self-interest),
 (iii) continuity, (iv) divisibility, (v) perfect competition, (vi) complete
 information, etc.
 If these conditions are not satisfied, we may not obtain the existence
 of a competitive equilibrium.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The conditions for the first fundamental Theorem of Welfare Economics are:
 (i) local non-satiation (unlimited desirability), (ii) divisibility, (iii)
 no externalities, (iv) perfect competition, (v) complete information, etc.
 if these conditions are not satisfied, we may not guarantee that every
 competitive equilibrium allocation is Pareto efficient.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The conditions for the Second Fundamental Theorem of Welfare Economics are:
 (i) the convexity of preferences and production sets, (ii) monotonicity
 (self-interest), (iii) continuity, (iv) divisibility, (v) perfect competition,
 (vi) complete information, etc.
 If these conditions are not satisfied, we may not guarantee every Pareto
 efficient allocation can be supported by a competitive equilibrium with
 transfers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Thus, as a general notice, before making an economic statement, one should
 pay attention to the assumptions which are implicitly and/or explicitly
 involved.
 As for a conclusion from the general equilibrium theory, one should notice
 conditions such as divisibility, no externalities, no increasing returns
 to scale, perfect competition, complete information.
 If these assumptions are relaxed, a competitive equilibrium may not exist
 or may not be Pareto efficient, or a Pareto efficient allocation may not
 be supported by a competitive equilibrium with transfer payments.
 Only in this case of a market failure, we may adopt another economic institutio
n.
\end_layout

